1
00:00:00,330 --> 00:00:01,304
In its final week,

2
00:00:01,354 --> 00:00:05,286
Maryland moved six hundred and fifty five prisoners out of one prison.

3
00:00:05,336 --> 00:00:07,107
Some of the buses left at night.

4
00:00:07,157 --> 00:00:10,976
Most of the officers working there were not told until the very end.

5
00:00:12,326 --> 00:00:15,520
Then, on a Monday morning in March two thousand seven,

6
00:00:15,570 --> 00:00:21,588
the governor announced that one of the oldest maximum security prisons in America was already closed.

7
00:00:21,638 --> 00:00:22,309
Empty. Done.

8
00:00:23,059 --> 00:00:26,336
Why did a state need a secret operation to close its own prison?

9
00:00:26,386 --> 00:00:28,467
And what was so dangerous about this one,

10
00:00:28,517 --> 00:00:31,950
that officials feared somebody could get killed if the news leaked?

11
00:00:32,920 --> 00:00:34,451
Chapter 1. The Cut.

12
00:00:36,581 --> 00:00:41,088
Jessup, Maryland, sits halfway between Baltimore and Washington.

13
00:00:41,138 --> 00:00:46,216
Today it's known for warehouses, highways, and prisons. Lots of prisons.

14
00:00:46,966 --> 00:00:49,990
The oldest of them was the Maryland House of Correction.

15
00:00:50,040 --> 00:00:52,570
It opened at the end of the eighteen seventies.

16
00:00:52,620 --> 00:00:54,381
Sources differ on the exact year,

17
00:00:54,431 --> 00:00:58,278
eighteen seventy eight or eighteen seventy nine. By two thousand seven,

18
00:00:58,328 --> 00:01:03,383
it was one of the oldest prisons in the United States still holding maximum security inmates.

19
00:01:04,133 --> 00:01:06,658
It was a big red brick building on a hill.

20
00:01:06,708 --> 00:01:08,558
According to one local history,

21
00:01:08,608 --> 00:01:11,561
prisoners helped make the bricks from local clay.

22
00:01:12,311 --> 00:01:13,843
Everyone called it the Cut.

23
00:01:13,893 --> 00:01:18,356
The name came from a cut dug through the countryside for the railroad nearby,

24
00:01:18,406 --> 00:01:22,340
known locally as Jessup's Cut. To generations of Maryland prisoners,

25
00:01:22,390 --> 00:01:24,567
being sent to the Cut meant one thing.

26
00:01:24,617 --> 00:01:26,325
The worst place in the system.

27
00:01:27,075 --> 00:01:31,795
It became so well known that its nickname turned up in the HBO series The Wire,

28
00:01:31,845 --> 00:01:32,822
set in Baltimore.

29
00:01:32,872 --> 00:01:35,780
Many viewers never knew the Cut was a real place.

30
00:01:36,530 --> 00:01:38,999
And by the start of the twenty first century,

31
00:01:39,049 --> 00:01:40,846
the real place was falling apart.

32
00:01:42,196 --> 00:01:46,568
Maryland had other old prisons, like the state penitentiary in Baltimore.

33
00:01:46,618 --> 00:01:48,688
But by the two thousands, officers,

34
00:01:48,738 --> 00:01:53,959
unions and lawmakers kept pointing to one building as the most dangerous in the system.

35
00:01:54,009 --> 00:01:54,443
The Cut.

36
00:01:55,413 --> 00:01:57,814
Chapter 2. Built for another century.

37
00:01:59,944 --> 00:02:04,396
The House of Correction was designed in the age of horses and steam trains.

38
00:02:04,446 --> 00:02:05,236
And it showed.

39
00:02:05,986 --> 00:02:10,305
Tight corners. Narrow staircases. Multi tier cell blocks with blind spots,

40
00:02:10,355 --> 00:02:14,496
where officers could not see what was happening just a few meters away.

41
00:02:15,246 --> 00:02:16,156
In some hallways,

42
00:02:16,206 --> 00:02:20,225
the layout forced officers to walk within arm's reach of inmates' cells.

43
00:02:20,275 --> 00:02:23,785
The walls were so thick that radios did not work in some areas.

44
00:02:23,835 --> 00:02:27,006
An officer in trouble might not be able to call for help.

45
00:02:27,756 --> 00:02:28,851
And the locks were old.

46
00:02:28,901 --> 00:02:30,993
Inmates had learned how to jimmy them open.

47
00:02:31,743 --> 00:02:35,659
Running a building like that was expensive. According to the state,

48
00:02:35,709 --> 00:02:36,961
in two thousand three,

49
00:02:37,011 --> 00:02:41,637
it cost eighty six dollars a day to hold one inmate at the House of Correction.

50
00:02:41,687 --> 00:02:45,781
The average across Maryland's prisons was sixty three. The difference,

51
00:02:45,831 --> 00:02:49,806
the state said, came from trying to provide security in an outdated,

52
00:02:49,856 --> 00:02:51,109
multi tiered building.

53
00:02:51,859 --> 00:02:53,537
As recently as two thousand six,

54
00:02:53,587 --> 00:02:56,940
the prison held more than twelve hundred men on an average day.

55
00:02:57,690 --> 00:03:01,658
It had a long history of trouble. Riots in nineteen forty five,

56
00:03:01,708 --> 00:03:06,377
nineteen sixty four, and, according to some sources, nineteen seventy two.

57
00:03:06,427 --> 00:03:11,415
In nineteen seventy nine, thirty inmates escaped after sawing through a window.

58
00:03:11,465 --> 00:03:16,198
In nineteen eighty eight, three escaped, and stayed free for thirteen days.

59
00:03:16,248 --> 00:03:20,726
During that time, they shot and killed a police officer in Tallahassee.

60
00:03:21,696 --> 00:03:24,341
Chapter 3. July 2006.

61
00:03:26,471 --> 00:03:28,440
In the summer of two thousand six,

62
00:03:28,490 --> 00:03:32,714
the problems inside the House of Correction became impossible to ignore.

63
00:03:33,464 --> 00:03:36,773
On the night of July twenty fifth, two thousand six,

64
00:03:36,823 --> 00:03:40,520
Corporal David McGuinn was doing a count in the west wing.

65
00:03:40,570 --> 00:03:43,169
He was forty two years old. He was alone.

66
00:03:43,919 --> 00:03:46,857
According to officials, at about ten in the evening,

67
00:03:46,907 --> 00:03:50,305
several inmates jumped him. They had got out of their cells,

68
00:03:50,355 --> 00:03:52,316
possibly by jimmying the old locks.

69
00:03:52,366 --> 00:03:54,615
He was stabbed in the neck and the body.

70
00:03:54,665 --> 00:03:55,994
He died from his wounds.

71
00:03:57,344 --> 00:03:59,214
According to the Washington Post,

72
00:03:59,264 --> 00:04:04,101
he was the first officer killed inside a Maryland prison since nineteen eighty four.

73
00:04:04,151 --> 00:04:08,173
And he was not the only Maryland corrections officer to die that year.

74
00:04:08,223 --> 00:04:08,813
In January,

75
00:04:08,863 --> 00:04:14,282
officer Jeffery Wroten had been shot by an inmate he was guarding at a hospital in Hagerstown.

76
00:04:15,032 --> 00:04:18,937
The inmate, Brandon Morris, had taken the officer's own revolver.

77
00:04:18,987 --> 00:04:20,824
He was later sentenced to life.

78
00:04:20,874 --> 00:04:24,049
Two Maryland corrections officers dead in six months.

79
00:04:24,099 --> 00:04:26,422
The pressure on the state was enormous.

80
00:04:27,172 --> 00:04:29,929
Two inmates, Lee Edward Stephens and Lamarr Harris,

81
00:04:29,979 --> 00:04:34,112
were charged with first degree murder. Prosecutors sought the death penalty.

82
00:04:34,862 --> 00:04:39,191
The case dragged on for years. According to reports, evidence went missing,

83
00:04:39,241 --> 00:04:43,569
and a prison captain was suspected of having walked out with crucial items.

84
00:04:43,619 --> 00:04:48,823
The state's key witness was a fellow inmate with a plea deal in an unrelated federal case.

85
00:04:48,873 --> 00:04:52,092
The defense argued Stephens had been wrongly identified.

86
00:04:52,842 --> 00:04:55,703
It also became the first test of a new Maryland law,

87
00:04:55,753 --> 00:04:59,343
which allowed the death penalty only when there was DNA evidence,

88
00:04:59,393 --> 00:05:03,262
a videotaped confession, or video linking the defendant to the murder.

89
00:05:03,312 --> 00:05:04,213
In twenty twelve,

90
00:05:04,263 --> 00:05:07,293
the jury found that DNA linked Stephens to the killing.

91
00:05:07,343 --> 00:05:10,428
But in the end, he was sentenced to life without parole,

92
00:05:10,478 --> 00:05:10,988
not death.

93
00:05:11,738 --> 00:05:14,591
After McGuinn's death, lawmakers held hearings.

94
00:05:14,641 --> 00:05:20,150
Top corrections officials described too few experienced officers, and too little training.

95
00:05:20,200 --> 00:05:24,289
They said inmates used smuggled cell phones to coordinate violence,

96
00:05:24,339 --> 00:05:28,119
plan attacks on officers, and run drug businesses from inside.

97
00:05:28,869 --> 00:05:32,214
One union leader said that of all the prisons in Maryland,

98
00:05:32,264 --> 00:05:36,020
the House of Correction was by far the most dangerous. For years,

99
00:05:36,070 --> 00:05:39,942
officers, unions and lawmakers had been asking for it to be closed.

100
00:05:40,692 --> 00:05:42,939
The state's answer, at first, was slow.

101
00:05:42,989 --> 00:05:47,239
Officials decided to convert the prison from maximum to minimum security.

102
00:05:47,289 --> 00:05:50,243
A process that could take, according to one report,

103
00:05:50,293 --> 00:05:51,303
up to three years.

104
00:05:52,053 --> 00:05:52,703
Three years.

105
00:05:52,753 --> 00:05:57,018
In a building where an officer had just been killed doing a routine count.

106
00:05:57,068 --> 00:06:00,342
For the people working inside, that was a very long time.

107
00:06:01,312 --> 00:06:03,411
Chapter 4. The new secretary.

108
00:06:05,541 --> 00:06:10,440
In January two thousand seven, Maryland got a new governor, Martin O'Malley.

109
00:06:10,490 --> 00:06:13,891
And a new man in charge of its prisons. Gary Maynard,

110
00:06:13,941 --> 00:06:16,366
who had run the prison system in Iowa,

111
00:06:16,416 --> 00:06:20,273
became secretary of public safety and correctional services.

112
00:06:21,023 --> 00:06:24,839
On February sixth, Maynard toured the House of Correction for himself.

113
00:06:25,589 --> 00:06:27,138
He did not like what he saw.

114
00:06:27,188 --> 00:06:31,591
I had never seen an institution that housed maximum security inmates that bad,

115
00:06:31,641 --> 00:06:32,390
he later said.

116
00:06:33,940 --> 00:06:34,595
The next day,

117
00:06:34,645 --> 00:06:38,280
he told the governor that the conversion would not take three years.

118
00:06:38,330 --> 00:06:40,068
He wanted it done in thirty days.

119
00:06:40,818 --> 00:06:44,998
Then, on March second, another officer was stabbed inside the prison.

120
00:06:45,048 --> 00:06:48,308
He survived. But for Maynard, it settled the question.

121
00:06:48,358 --> 00:06:51,680
He no longer wanted to convert the House of Correction.

122
00:06:51,730 --> 00:06:52,906
He wanted it closed.

123
00:06:53,656 --> 00:06:57,021
When I took this job, he said later, my top priority,

124
00:06:57,071 --> 00:07:00,435
to protect the public, our employees and the inmates,

125
00:07:00,485 --> 00:07:02,174
was to close this facility.

126
00:07:03,144 --> 00:07:04,826
Chapter 5. Need to know.

127
00:07:06,956 --> 00:07:12,835
Closing a prison is normally a public process. Announcements. Hearings. Plans.

128
00:07:12,885 --> 00:07:15,572
Months of preparation, in full view.

129
00:07:16,322 --> 00:07:18,176
Maynard decided to do the opposite.

130
00:07:19,726 --> 00:07:20,876
The fear was simple.

131
00:07:20,926 --> 00:07:24,057
If the inmates found out that the prison was closing,

132
00:07:24,107 --> 00:07:27,537
and that they were about to be scattered across the state,

133
00:07:27,587 --> 00:07:31,618
and even across the country, there could be violence. Fights. Riots.

134
00:07:31,668 --> 00:07:33,718
Attacks on staff in the final days.

135
00:07:34,468 --> 00:07:37,043
So the plan was kept on a need to know basis.

136
00:07:37,093 --> 00:07:38,967
According to the Washington Post,

137
00:07:39,017 --> 00:07:42,233
only five of the secretary's twelve top aides were told.

138
00:07:42,283 --> 00:07:44,916
Some of them only found out in the final week.

139
00:07:45,666 --> 00:07:48,703
Maynard explained it bluntly. All of us knew, he said,

140
00:07:48,753 --> 00:07:51,847
that if we told someone who didn't have a need to know,

141
00:07:51,897 --> 00:07:55,219
that would potentially be the person to get someone killed.

142
00:07:56,769 --> 00:07:57,746
Behind the scenes,

143
00:07:57,796 --> 00:08:03,680
the Division of Correction carried out a huge reclassification of inmates across the whole state system.

144
00:08:03,730 --> 00:08:07,047
Who could go where. Who was too dangerous for which prison.

145
00:08:07,097 --> 00:08:09,215
Who needed to leave Maryland entirely.

146
00:08:09,965 --> 00:08:13,207
Then they started finding beds. Refurbishing empty cells.

147
00:08:13,257 --> 00:08:17,538
Moving other inmates around to make room. According to the Washington Post,

148
00:08:17,588 --> 00:08:24,525
around one hundred and fifty empty cells across the state were fixed up and filled with men from the House of Correction.

149
00:08:25,495 --> 00:08:27,397
Chapter 6. The buses.

150
00:08:29,527 --> 00:08:33,189
The transfers began weeks before the announcement. Quietly.

151
00:08:33,239 --> 00:08:34,762
A few at a time at first.

152
00:08:35,512 --> 00:08:38,013
Inmates were loaded onto buses by the dozens.

153
00:08:38,063 --> 00:08:41,074
Some of the buses left at night. According to reports,

154
00:08:41,124 --> 00:08:45,496
many prisoners only learned where they were going after they had already left.

155
00:08:46,246 --> 00:08:51,088
They went everywhere. To other prisons in Jessup. To Baltimore. To Hagerstown.

156
00:08:51,138 --> 00:08:55,980
To Westover, on Maryland's Eastern Shore. And several hundred to North Branch,

157
00:08:56,030 --> 00:08:59,242
a brand new maximum security prison near Cumberland,

158
00:08:59,292 --> 00:09:01,562
in the mountains of western Maryland.

159
00:09:02,312 --> 00:09:05,014
And some went much further. Ninety seven inmates,

160
00:09:05,064 --> 00:09:08,719
described by the state as among the most disruptive in the system,

161
00:09:08,769 --> 00:09:10,685
were sent out of Maryland entirely.

162
00:09:10,735 --> 00:09:13,324
Some to state prisons in Virginia and Kentucky.

163
00:09:13,374 --> 00:09:15,850
Others to federal prisons across the country.

164
00:09:16,600 --> 00:09:18,460
They were chosen, the state said,

165
00:09:18,510 --> 00:09:23,089
based on their history of assaults and serious behavior problems, gang activity,

166
00:09:23,139 --> 00:09:27,718
and their continuing negative influence on the community. Intelligence officers,

167
00:09:27,768 --> 00:09:30,437
prison staff and local police helped pick them.

168
00:09:31,187 --> 00:09:33,473
In return for taking some of those inmates,

169
00:09:33,523 --> 00:09:37,058
Maryland agreed to house sixty female prisoners from out of state.

170
00:09:37,808 --> 00:09:38,226
In total,

171
00:09:38,276 --> 00:09:41,555
more than eight hundred inmates were moved in less than a month.

172
00:09:42,305 --> 00:09:46,119
And in the final week, the pace became extraordinary. On Monday,

173
00:09:46,169 --> 00:09:48,171
March twelfth, two thousand seven,

174
00:09:48,221 --> 00:09:52,156
the House of Correction still held six hundred and fifty five men.

175
00:09:52,206 --> 00:09:55,295
By the end of that week, every one of them was gone.

176
00:09:56,845 --> 00:10:00,336
Think about what that meant in practice. Hundreds of prisoners,

177
00:10:00,386 --> 00:10:04,158
many of them violent, each needing a new cell, a new classification,

178
00:10:04,208 --> 00:10:05,676
a seat on a bus, an escort,

179
00:10:05,726 --> 00:10:07,811
and a receiving prison ready for them.

180
00:10:07,861 --> 00:10:11,914
All while the people inside were not supposed to know what was happening.

181
00:10:12,884 --> 00:10:14,554
Chapter 7. Friday.

182
00:10:16,684 --> 00:10:20,930
The officers who worked inside the House of Correction were among the last to know.

183
00:10:21,680 --> 00:10:24,842
According to the Washington Post, they were told on Friday.

184
00:10:24,892 --> 00:10:26,692
All of them would keep their jobs.

185
00:10:26,742 --> 00:10:29,632
But the prison they knew as the Cut was shutting down,

186
00:10:29,682 --> 00:10:30,340
as of Monday.

187
00:10:31,090 --> 00:10:33,517
Many of them had worked there for years.

188
00:10:33,567 --> 00:10:36,428
Some had walked those narrow tiers for decades.

189
00:10:36,478 --> 00:10:40,267
They had seen colleagues stabbed. One of them killed. And now,

190
00:10:40,317 --> 00:10:41,877
it was over, in a weekend.

191
00:10:43,227 --> 00:10:45,549
One union leader, a lieutenant at the prison,

192
00:10:45,599 --> 00:10:48,553
said there would be adjustments staff would have to make.

193
00:10:48,603 --> 00:10:52,559
He also compared the old building to the prison in The Shawshank Redemption.

194
00:10:52,609 --> 00:10:53,402
It was, he said,

195
00:10:53,452 --> 00:10:56,090
what a person would dream a prison would look like.

196
00:10:56,840 --> 00:10:59,014
Officers' unions welcomed the closure.

197
00:10:59,064 --> 00:11:02,056
A legislative director for the union called it huge,

198
00:11:02,106 --> 00:11:07,322
and said moving officers to understaffed prisons nearby would be a huge secondary benefit.

199
00:11:08,072 --> 00:11:10,188
But not everyone was happy with the secrecy.

200
00:11:10,238 --> 00:11:15,605
A prisoners' advocacy group worried that families were learning where their relatives were from the newspaper.

201
00:11:16,575 --> 00:11:18,105
Chapter 8. The leak.

202
00:11:20,235 --> 00:11:20,857
In the end,

203
00:11:20,907 --> 00:11:24,643
the secret did not quite hold until the official announcement.

204
00:11:25,393 --> 00:11:27,066
On Sunday, March eighteenth,

205
00:11:27,116 --> 00:11:31,125
the Baltimore Sun reported the closure and the transfers. By then,

206
00:11:31,175 --> 00:11:33,647
it hardly mattered. The prison was empty.

207
00:11:34,397 --> 00:11:37,019
On Monday, March nineteenth, two thousand seven,

208
00:11:37,069 --> 00:11:43,809
Governor Martin O'Malley and Secretary Gary Maynard officially announced that the Maryland House of Correction was closed.

209
00:11:44,559 --> 00:11:49,034
The press release was headlined, Maryland House of Correction is closed.

210
00:11:49,084 --> 00:11:49,977
And underneath,

211
00:11:50,027 --> 00:11:57,456
six hundred and fifty five inmates moved in one week to shut down nineteenth century maximum security prison in Jessup.

212
00:11:58,206 --> 00:12:00,716
This was a bold initiative, the governor said.

213
00:12:00,766 --> 00:12:04,780
We said from day one that this was a problem that needed to be addressed,

214
00:12:04,830 --> 00:12:05,448
and quickly.

215
00:12:06,198 --> 00:12:10,758
This facility, Maynard said, is not suited for modern day incarceration,

216
00:12:10,808 --> 00:12:12,487
much less maximum security.

217
00:12:13,237 --> 00:12:21,674
One national corrections expert told the Washington Post that you would be hard pressed to find a prison anywhere in the country that had recently been so plagued with violence.

218
00:12:22,424 --> 00:12:25,845
There were no public reports of violence during the transfers.

219
00:12:25,895 --> 00:12:29,260
As far as is publicly known, the secret operation had worked.

220
00:12:30,810 --> 00:12:33,116
For the state, it was a rare success story.

221
00:12:33,166 --> 00:12:37,826
Maryland's corrections system had spent months in the headlines for the wrong reasons.

222
00:12:37,876 --> 00:12:44,509
Now its leaders could say they had moved hundreds of the most dangerous men in the state without a single public incident.

223
00:12:45,479 --> 00:12:47,427
Chapter 9. The empty prison.

224
00:12:49,557 --> 00:12:53,846
On March twenty second, two thousand seven, three days after the announcement,

225
00:12:53,896 --> 00:12:57,462
staff from the Maryland State Archives toured the empty building,

226
00:12:57,512 --> 00:13:01,468
and photographed the cell blocks, the cells, and the disciplinary block.

227
00:13:01,518 --> 00:13:05,751
The State of Maryland was documenting its own history, before it disappeared.

228
00:13:06,501 --> 00:13:08,967
The hospital on the grounds stayed open,

229
00:13:09,017 --> 00:13:13,559
serving inmates from the other Jessup prisons. A few employees stayed on,

230
00:13:13,609 --> 00:13:17,019
doing inventories. The rest of the building was silent.

231
00:13:17,769 --> 00:13:20,767
For years, the state did not decide what to do with it.

232
00:13:20,817 --> 00:13:22,097
In August twenty twelve,

233
00:13:22,147 --> 00:13:25,644
it opened the old prison to the public for a two day open house.

234
00:13:25,694 --> 00:13:28,914
More than two thousand people came to walk through the Cut.

235
00:13:29,664 --> 00:13:32,770
Then, in January twenty fourteen, the cranes arrived.

236
00:13:32,820 --> 00:13:35,629
The Maryland House of Correction was demolished.

237
00:13:36,979 --> 00:13:39,086
But the story of Jessup was not over.

238
00:13:39,136 --> 00:13:41,476
The other prisons around it kept running.

239
00:13:41,526 --> 00:13:45,440
And in September twenty twenty five, Maryland's governor, Wes Moore,

240
00:13:45,490 --> 00:13:48,296
announced that another prison on the same campus,

241
00:13:48,346 --> 00:13:52,085
the Maryland Correctional Institution at Jessup, would close too,

242
00:13:52,135 --> 00:13:54,242
by the end of June twenty twenty six.

243
00:13:54,992 --> 00:13:58,908
Once again, around seven hundred men had to be moved. Once again,

244
00:13:58,958 --> 00:14:01,837
officials said no employee would lose their job.

245
00:14:01,887 --> 00:14:05,193
Staff were to be reassigned to other prisons in Jessup,

246
00:14:05,243 --> 00:14:07,878
including the women's prison and Dorsey Run.

247
00:14:08,628 --> 00:14:13,988
The reasons sounded familiar. An aging building. Expensive repairs. According to reports,

248
00:14:14,038 --> 00:14:17,940
the prison needed around two hundred million dollars in upgrades.

249
00:14:17,990 --> 00:14:22,620
The state said closing it would save about twenty one million dollars a year.

250
00:14:23,370 --> 00:14:25,376
And the inmates were moved in phases,

251
00:14:25,426 --> 00:14:29,264
starting with men serving life sentences and people with disabilities,

252
00:14:29,314 --> 00:14:33,375
to prisons in Hagerstown, to Roxbury, and to North Branch near Cumberland.

253
00:14:33,425 --> 00:14:37,208
The same mountain prison that had taken hundreds of men from the Cut,

254
00:14:37,258 --> 00:14:38,763
almost twenty years earlier.

255
00:14:39,513 --> 00:14:42,200
But this time, there were no secret night buses.

256
00:14:42,250 --> 00:14:44,537
The plan was announced nine months ahead,

257
00:14:44,587 --> 00:14:47,046
with a public list of questions and answers.

258
00:14:47,096 --> 00:14:50,980
Maryland had learned that a prison closure could be done in the open.

259
00:14:51,030 --> 00:14:54,630
Or perhaps, this time, the building simply was not as dangerous.

260
00:14:55,600 --> 00:14:57,629
Chapter 10. Why the secret?

261
00:14:59,759 --> 00:15:03,134
So why did Maryland need a secret operation to close one prison?

262
00:15:03,884 --> 00:15:09,508
Because the building was so dangerous that officials believed the news alone could get someone killed.

263
00:15:09,558 --> 00:15:13,848
Because the inmates inside included some of the most violent men in the state.

264
00:15:13,898 --> 00:15:17,909
Because an officer had already died there, alone, during a routine count.

265
00:15:17,959 --> 00:15:22,693
And because the system had spent years talking about the problem instead of fixing it.

266
00:15:23,443 --> 00:15:26,794
In the end, a new secretary, a small circle of officials,

267
00:15:26,844 --> 00:15:31,508
and a fleet of buses did in a month what the state had failed to do in decades.

268
00:15:33,058 --> 00:15:34,623
Was it the right way to do it?

269
00:15:34,673 --> 00:15:37,799
The families who found out from the newspaper might say no.

270
00:15:37,849 --> 00:15:41,405
The officers who never had to walk those tiers again might say yes.

271
00:15:42,155 --> 00:15:44,811
So what do you think? Was the secrecy necessary,

272
00:15:44,861 --> 00:15:48,024
or should the public have known? Tell us in the comments.

273
00:15:48,074 --> 00:15:52,140
And if you want to see what happens when a prison is not emptied in time,

274
00:15:52,190 --> 00:15:54,000
watch our story about Lucasville,

275
00:15:54,050 --> 00:15:56,424
where inmates held a block for eleven days.
